From fae4f242b4a6b075ddfa57b48a98f8e4a7fe3937 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 23:25:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/11] Time each exported path as one motion, so the robot
 stops only where Pedro does

The preview stopped at the end of every segment. Each one had its own
accelerate / cruise / decelerate-to-zero profile, plus a turn-in-place
pause wherever headings met at an angle. That happened even inside a
group, which the code export writes as a single Pedro path. The robot
does not stop there. Pedro 3's follower slows only toward the end of the
path it is following, and holds at that end until the next follow().

calculatePathTime now splits the sequence the same way buildSequence
does: consecutive segments of one top-level path form one run, and a
wait ends it. It times each run as one profile over its total length.
Each segment still gets its own travel event, cut from that shared
profile, so everything that looks up the active segment keeps working.
The event also carries its place in the run, and calculateRobotState
reads position off the run's profile. Speed therefore carries through
joins inside a path. The turn-in-place check now applies only where the
robot starts from rest.

Measured with the app's own timeline and animation code on a sample with
a two-segment group: at the join, the robot went from 0.3 in/s (stopped)
to about 25 in/s, and playback went from 17.7 s to 16.8 s. Stops between
separate paths and at waits are unchanged, and those match the robot.
